ately, wasting a turn.  \n\nGiven your teammate has 17 cards, it’s reasonable to assume they might have a middle card to beat ♠5 without wasting top cards. Letting them play could save your high cards for later when landlord is closer to emptying.  \n\n**Reason:**  \n\nCooperating with your teammate means sometimes passing even when you can beat, to conserve high cards and let teammate gain control if possible. Since landlord only has 10 cards left, your priority should be to save your strongest cards (RedJoker, ♥2) for later critical moments when you need to stop landlord’s push. Here, passing risks landlord getting the turn, but if teammate can’t beat ♠5, landlord will play again anyway. Still, passing is acceptable because:  \n\n- Your RedJoker and ♥2 are too valuable to spend on a low ♠5.  \n- If teammate has any card above 5, they can beat it and possibly play a card that forces landlord to use higher cards.  \n- If teammate passes too, landlord gets a free play, but at least you haven’t revealed or wasted high cards early.  \n\nThus, I’ll pass.  \n\n**Answer:** {"cards": []}'}
